module std.coordinate_frame

import std.integer { Int }
import std.types { Bool }

// A COORDINATE FRAME'S IDENTITY, CARRIED BY THE VALUES THAT LIVE IN IT.
//
// This module exists because of a measurement, which is the whole justification for its shape.
// SPATIAL-2 modelled the frame as a phantom type parameter - `Point3<RoomFrame>` - on the stated
// ground that a cross-frame subtraction "does not typecheck", called the stronger of the two
// options. Measured on the production compile-and-run path on 2026-08-11, it is false: a
// `Point3<RoomFrame>` and a `Point3<BuildingFrame>` flowed through `displacement_between3` and the
// program compiled clean and returned 9. Not a refusal, not a crash - a plausible number for a
// question with no answer, precisely the fabricated output DESIGN 5 forbids.
//
// The cause is a v1 seed limitation, not a modelling slip. `v1.compiler.infer`'s argument-position
// judge compares nominal HEAD NAMES; applied type arguments live in the type node's children and
// every name-level comparison discards them. `Point3` equals `Point3`, so the frames were never
// compared. The repair to that judge (`applied_nominal_call_arg_mismatch`) lands alongside this
// module and closes the call-boundary case - but CANNOT close the producer side, because
// return-position conformance is a separate relation that declines to judge record types at all,
// blocked on `feature:conformance-produced-type-identity`. So this stays writable regardless:
//
//     fn falsely_reframe(p: Point3<RoomFrame>) -> Point3<BuildingFrame> { p }
//
// A consumer receiving that value sees only the declared type and cannot reconstruct an identity
// the producer already erased. THEREFORE THE CARRIED IDENTITY IS THE AUTHORITY, and the honest
// claim is frame consistency BY CHECKED DERIVATION, not by construction. Every frame-sensitive
// operation compares the identities its operands carry and returns a typed mismatch refusal -
// which survives a lying return annotation, because the value still says where it came from.
//
// WHY NOT KEEP THE PHANTOM TOO. Two authorities for one fact is the DESIGN 3 violation whichever
// is right, and the weaker here is the one the compiler cannot enforce end-to-end. A phantom
// retained beside an authoritative carried identity invites exactly the reading that just cost a
// merged PR its central claim: that the type parameter is doing work. It is removed, not demoted.
// Dissolve-on: when `feature:conformance-produced-type-identity` closes, a static frame projection
// can be reintroduced ABOVE this carrier and promoted to the construction rung, with the carried
// check remaining as the serialization / external-input boundary a static guarantee cannot reach.
//
// IDENTITY IS MINTED AND DECLARATION-OWNED, never derived from a name, a path, or the frame's
// contents, following the ruling recorded for `gunbc.host_resource` `HostResourceIdentity`: a
// content-derived key makes two coincidentally-identical frames one frame, the heuristic DESIGN 4
// rules unnecessary in a closed system. A frame's human-facing name is a display fact belonging on
// whatever registers frames; it is deliberately absent here so it cannot be mistaken for identity.
type CoordinateFrameIdentity {
  occurrence: Int
}

// THIRD INSTANCE OF ONE SHAPE, recorded rather than abstracted. `std.occurrence_identity` mints
// SOURCE-occurrence identities, `gunbc.host_resource` mints host-resource identities
// (HostResourceIdentity), and this mints coordinate-frame identities; all three are "a minted Int
// occurrence in a subject-specific space". The subject-parameterized carrier that would unify them
// is a registered trigger on the fleet-reconcile (membership-diff reconcile spine) lane, and stays
// a trigger rather than being built here: sharing one Int space across subjects would let
// `coordinate_frame_identity_eq` answer true on a numeric coincidence with an unrelated subject,
// the cross-family comparison the ContentHash family grounding closed. Do not merge the Int spaces
// without the subject parameter. What this row adds is evidence the extraction is due - three
// subjects, not two.

fn coordinate_frame_identity(occurrence: Int) -> CoordinateFrameIdentity {
  CoordinateFrameIdentity { occurrence: occurrence }
}

fn coordinate_frame_identity_eq(a: CoordinateFrameIdentity, b: CoordinateFrameIdentity) -> Bool {
  a.occurrence == b.occurrence
}
